course: biology
lesson: 313
title: Whales by kind, the toothed whales: sperm whale, orca, beluga and beaked whales
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Toothed whales hunt one animal at a time instead of sieving the sea. They have one blowhole, and most find their food by sending out clicks and listening for the echoes.
explain: The sperm whale, with its huge square head, dives into the black deep to hunt squid; the orca is the biggest dolphin; the white beluga chirps among the Arctic ice.
explain: The strangest are the beaked whales: deep divers so shy that some kinds are known only from a few bodies washed up on beaches.
explain: Think of Moby Dick, the white whale in the old story: he was a sperm whale, and real sperm whale skin often shows round scars from the suckers of squid they fought in the dark.
fact: The sperm whale has the biggest brain of any animal, about 8 kilograms; its huge head works like a sound gun for very loud clicks and was once hunted for its oil, burned in lamps.
fact: Sperm whales dive over 2 kilometres deep, often for an hour or more, hunting squid; they store oxygen in their blood and muscles.
fact: Sperm whales nap upright near the surface for short spells, often about a quarter of an hour; in 2008 scientists drifted into a group of sleeping sperm whales that did not wake.
fact: A sperm whale has about 40 to 52 big teeth, all in the lower jaw, which fit into sockets in the upper jaw; because they swallow squid whole, what the teeth are for is still argued.
fact: Ambergris is a waxy lump that forms in a sperm whale's gut around hard squid beaks and can wash ashore; it was used in perfume for hundreds of years, and some countries now ban selling it.
fact: The orca, or killer whale, is the biggest member of the dolphin family; different groups eat different food, some only fish, others seals or even other whales.
fact: There are no confirmed cases of wild orcas killing a person.
fact: Since 2020 a group of orcas off Spain and Portugal has been pushing and biting the rudders of sailing boats and has sunk a few; scientists are not sure why, maybe play or a fashion copied between orcas.
fact: The beluga lives in the Arctic, where its white skin hides it among the ice; sailors called belugas sea canaries for their whistles and chirps.
fact: The beluga's bulgy forehead, the melon, changes shape as it makes sounds, and its seven neck bones are not joined together, so it can nod and turn its head.
fact: There are about 24 kinds of beaked whale; Cuvier's beaked whale holds the record dive for a mammal, about 3 kilometres deep and over 3 hours long.
fact: Most beaked whales have almost no teeth; males have just two or four, and the male strap-toothed whale's two teeth grow up over its upper jaw like straps, so it sucks squid in like a vacuum.
exercise: About how heavy is a sperm whale's brain? | About 8 kilograms, the biggest of any animal | About 1 kilogram, the same as a person's | About 80 kilograms, half of its whole body weight | About 8 grams, like a mouse's
exercise: What does a sperm whale's huge head help it do? | Make very loud clicks | Store fresh water for long trips | Float without ever swimming | Hold its babies safe inside
exercise: How deep can sperm whales dive? | Over 2 kilometres | About 20 metres, no deeper | About 200 kilometres | Never below the surface
exercise: What do sperm whales hunt in the deep? | Squid | Krill and seaweed | Seals on the ice | Sea birds
exercise: How do sperm whales sleep? | Upright near the surface in short naps | Lying flat on the seabed for a whole long night | On beaches out of the water, in the sun | They never sleep at all, not even for a minute
exercise: Where are a sperm whale's big teeth? | Only in the lower jaw | Only in the upper jaw | On its tongue | Hidden in its blowhole
exercise: What is ambergris? | A waxy lump formed in a sperm whale's gut around squid beaks | A type of seaweed that floats ashore after storms | Whale fat melted down by sailors | A black rock thrown up by undersea volcanoes near the coast of islands
exercise: Which family does the orca belong to? | The dolphin family; it is the biggest dolphin | The shark family | The seal and sea lion family | The family of the great baleen whales
exercise: Do all orcas eat the same food? | No, some groups eat only fish, others seals or whales | Yes, all orcas everywhere eat only penguins and nothing else | Yes, all eat only krill | No, some eat only seaweed and sea grass
exercise: What have some orcas off Spain and Portugal been doing since 2020? | Pushing and biting the rudders of sailing boats | Singing loud songs to the fishing boats at night | Carrying tired swimmers back to shore | Building nests of seaweed
exercise: Why is the beluga white? | White hides it among the Arctic ice | It turned white from living in very cold water | It is a very old whale | It eats only white fish
exercise: Why did sailors call belugas sea canaries? | Because of their whistles and chirps | Because they are bright yellow | Because they fly over the waves like birds | Because they live in cages
exercise: Why can a beluga turn its head when most whales cannot? | Its neck bones are not joined together | It has no neck bones at all, only muscle | It has twice as many neck bones as we have | Its head is not joined to its body at all
exercise: How many neck bones does a beluga have? | Seven, the same as you | Only one, just like a fish | Seventy, like a long snake | None, whales have no necks
exercise: Why are some kinds of beaked whale known only from bodies washed up on beaches? | They are shy deep divers living far from shore | They only live in aquariums | They died out a million years ago, before people | They only come out on land to rest in the sun
exercise: What record does Cuvier's beaked whale hold? | The deepest and longest dive of any mammal | The loudest song in the sea | The fastest swim of any whale in the ocean | The longest teeth of any animal ever found
exercise: How does a male strap-toothed whale eat with teeth growing over its mouth? | It sucks squid in like a vacuum | It chews its food with its tongue | It stops eating once it is an adult | It eats through its blowhole instead
test: Count them: a sperm whale has 50 teeth in its lower jaw. How many in its upper jaw? | None; the upper jaw only has sockets | 50, the same as below | 25, half as many as below | 100, twice as many, in two rows
test: Count them: a male strap-toothed whale and a male with four teeth meet. How many teeth do they have together? | 6 | 8 | 4 | 2
test: Count them: 2 sperm whales and 3 belugas surface. All are toothed whales. How many blowholes do you see? | 5 | 10 | 3 | 7
test: In Free Willy an orca is called a killer whale and the people are scared of it. What is true? | No wild orca is confirmed to have killed a person, and it is really a dolphin | Orcas hunt people every day in every ocean | Orcas are a kind of shark | Orcas are small baleen whales that eat krill
test: «lol just found a smelly waxy lump on the beach, can i sell it» What should you know? | It might be ambergris; some countries ban selling it to protect whales | It is always plastic, throw it back in the sea | It is a sperm whale's egg | It is a lump of whale poo with real gold hidden inside it
test: What would happen if a sleeping sperm whale sank too deep to reach the air? | It would have to wake and swim up to breathe | It would breathe through its gills | It would sleep on the seabed for a year | It would float up by itself, with no need for any air
test: A mate says the orcas biting boats off Spain must be angry for revenge. What do scientists say? | They are not sure why; it may be play or a fashion copied between orcas | They are certainly angry at one boat that hurt them | They are trying to steal the fish | They think the boats are seals
test: «why do sperm whales have scars like circles all over» What is the best answer? | They are marks from the suckers of squid they hunt | They are bite marks from sharks only | They are sunburn spots | They are from whale lice nibbling at their skin
test: Why would a whale that hunts in the black deep need very loud clicks? | To find prey by echoes where there is no light | To scare away the light | To call sea birds down to help it hunt | To keep itself warm
test: In a cartoon a whale bends its neck to look behind it like a person. Which real whale comes closest? | The beluga, whose neck bones are not joined | The blue whale, which can turn its head right round | The sperm whale, which has a long neck | No whale; all whales have no neck bones
test: «the beluga's head changes shape?? creepy» What is happening? | Its melon changes shape as it makes sounds | Its skull is broken | It is breathing through its forehead | It is storing extra food in its soft forehead
exam: Identify the principal prey of the sperm whale and the depth at which it is pursued. | Squid, pursued at depths in excess of two kilometres | Krill, filtered at depths of a few metres | Seals, captured upon the surface of the Arctic ice | Seaweed, gathered from the shallows
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the orca. | It is the largest member of the dolphin family and its populations differ in diet | It is a species of shark which feeds exclusively upon persons | It is a baleen whale which feeds solely upon krill | It is a seal which hunts beneath the Arctic ice and breathes through holes it keeps open
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the beluga? | An Arctic whale whose unfused cervical vertebrae permit the head to turn | A tropical whale whose neck is wholly absent | A deep-sea fish whose forehead emits light | A whale of the Antarctic which lacks a blowhole and respires through the skin
exam: Ascertain the reason for which certain species of beaked whale are known principally from stranded carcasses. | They dwell far from shore and dive to great depths, and are therefore rarely observed alive | They became extinct before the invention of boats | They inhabit freshwater lakes inaccessible to scientists | They emerge from the sea only at night
